#doc (Manual) "Unitality and Other Topologies" =>

The results in this chapter are not needed for the proof that a $`W^*`-algebra has a unique
predual, and nothing outside this chapter depends on them. They record parts of Sakai's development that the formalization was able to avoid,
together with what has been formalized of them.

# Unitality of WStarAlgebras

Sakai does not assume that a $`W^*`-algebra has a unit; it is a consequence of the definition
({bpref "lem:wstar_unital"}[]), via the Krein–Milman theorem and the following characterization
of unital $`C^*`-algebras by the extreme points of their unit balls. The formalization assumes a
unit from the outset, and these results justify that choice.

:::lemma_ "lem:one_is_extreme" (lean := "CStarAlgebra.one_mem_extremePoints_unitClosedBall") (uses := "thm:comm_cstar_cts_fcns_Sak_1_2_1, lem:star_pres_norm_Sak_1_1_6")
In a unital $`C^*`-algebra $`\mathcal{A}`, the identity $`1` is an extreme point of the
closed unit sphere $`S`.
:::

:::proof "lem:one_is_extreme"
Suppose $`a, b \in S` and $`1 = \frac{a + b}{2}`. Then, since $`1 = 1^*`, we have
$`1 = \frac{a^* + b^*}{2}` and one checks that $`1 = \frac{c + d}{2}`, where
$`c = \frac{a + a^*}{2}` and $`d = \frac{b + b^*}{2}`. Now $`d = 2 - c` and so $`d` and $`c`
commute and each are self-adjoint (hence normal), and therefore $`C^*\{1, d, c\}` is
commutative and can be identified with $`C(K)` for a compact Hausdorff space $`K`. The
self-adjointness of $`c` and $`d`, the triangle inequality and
{bpref "lem:star_pres_norm_Sak_1_1_6"}[] imply that the ranges of the associated functions
$`f, g` are in $`[-1, 1]`. In order for $`f(t) + g(t) = 2` for all $`t \in K` it must be that
$`f(t)` and $`g(t)` are always $`1`, and thus $`c = d = 1`. Now $`2 = 2c = a + a^*` and so
$`a = 2 - a^*`, hence $`a` is normal and so the unital $`C^*`-subalgebra generated by $`a` is
commutative. Again represent the unital $`C^*`-algebra as $`C(K)` for some compact Hausdorff
$`K`. Let $`f` be the function corresponding to $`a`. We have $`\Re(f)(t) = 1` for all $`t`,
and hence $`\Re(f)(t)^2 = |f(t)| = 1` for all $`t` and $`\Im(f)(t) = 0` for all $`t`, and
therefore $`a = 1`.
:::

:::lemma_ "lem:fake_approx_unit" (uses := "cor:nonunital_gelfand_Sak_1_2_2")
Let $`x` be an extreme point of the norm unit ball of a $`C^*`-algebra $`\mathcal{A}`, so
that the $`C^*`-subalgebra generated by $`x^* x` is identified with $`C_0(\Omega)` as in
{bpref "cor:nonunital_gelfand_Sak_1_2_2"}[]. There exists a sequence $`(y_n)` of positive
elements in $`C_0(\Omega)` with $`\|y_n\| \le 1` for all $`n`, such that
$`\|(x^* x) y_n - x^* x\| \to 0` and $`\|(x^* x) y_n^2 - x^* x\| \to 0` as $`n \to \infty`.
:::

:::lemma_ "lem:extreme_partial_isom" (lean := "isStarProjection_star_mul_self_of_mem_extremePoints_unitClosedBall, isStarProjection_self_mul_star_of_mem_extremePoints_unitClosedBall") (uses := "def:cstar_def, lem:fake_approx_unit")
If $`x` is an extreme point of the norm unit ball of a $`C^*`-algebra $`\mathcal{A}`, then
$`x^* x` is a projection. That is, $`x` is a partial isometry.
:::

:::theorem "thm:cstar_unital_iff_extreme_Sak_1_6_1" (lean := "CStarAlgebra.isUnital_iff, CStarAlgebra.mul_ofExtremePtOne, CStarAlgebra.ofExtremePtOne_mul") (uses := "lem:one_is_extreme, lem:extreme_partial_isom")
(Sakai 1.6.1) A $`C^*`-algebra is unital if and only if its closed unit ball has an extreme
point.
:::

:::proposition "prop:extr_pos_projs_Sak_1_6_2" (tags := "mathlib") (lean := "isStarProjection_iff_mem_extremePoints_setOfPred_nonneg_inter_unitClosedBall") (uses := "def:cstar_def")
(Sakai 1.6.2) Let $`P` be the set of positive elements in a $`C^*`-algebra $`\mathcal{A}`
with unit sphere $`S`. Then the extreme points of $`P \cap S` are the projections of
$`\mathcal{A}`.
:::

:::proposition "prop:extr_sa_sa_unitaries_Sak_1_6_3" (lean := "mem_extremePoints_isSelfAdjoint_and_mem_unitClosedBall_iff_isSelfAdjoint_and_mem_unitary, selfAdjoint.mem_extremePoints_unitClosedBall_iff_coe_mem_unitary") (uses := "prop:extr_pos_projs_Sak_1_6_2")
(Sakai 1.6.3) Let $`\mathcal{A}^s` be the set of self-adjoint elements in a $`C^*`-algebra
$`\mathcal{A}` with unit sphere $`S`. Then the set of extreme points of
$`\mathcal{A}^s \cap S` is the set of all self-adjoint unitary elements of $`\mathcal{A}`.
:::

:::theorem "thm:krein_milman" (tags := "mathlib") (lean := "IsCompact.extremePoints_nonempty, closure_convexHull_extremePoints")
(Krein–Milman) A nonempty compact convex subset of a Hausdorff locally convex space has an
extreme point; indeed, it is the closed convex hull of its extreme points.
:::

:::lemma_ "lem:wstar_unital" (uses := "thm:cstar_unital_iff_extreme_Sak_1_6_1, thm:krein_milman, thm:Banach_Alaoglu, def:sigma_top")
Every $`W^*`-algebra is unital, even if it is not assumed to be so in {bpref "def:wstar_def"}[].
:::

:::proof "lem:wstar_unital"
The norm unit ball of $`M = (M_*)^*` is convex and
is compact by {bpref "thm:Banach_Alaoglu"}[], and so has an extreme point by the Krein–Milman
theorem ({bpref "thm:krein_milman"}[]). By
{bpref "thm:cstar_unital_iff_extreme_Sak_1_6_1"}[], the result follows.
:::

# WStarSubalgebras and the Projection Lattice

Sakai shows that the projections of a $`W^*`-algebra form a complete lattice by way of
$`W^*`-subalgebras and one-sided ideals. The proof of the main theorem only needs suprema of
directed sets of projections, which {bpref "lem:directed_sup_proj"}[] provides directly.

:::corollary "cor:sig_clos_of_cstar_is_wstar_Sak_1_7_9" (uses := "lem:left_mul_proj_right_mul_proj_sig_cts_Sak_1_7_7, lem:star_left_mul_right_mul_sig_cts_Sak_1_7_8, def:sigma_top")
(Sakai 1.7.9) Let $`H` be a subset of $`M` and $`\mathcal{A}` be the $`C^*`-subalgebra of
$`M` generated by $`H`, and let $`\overline{\mathcal{A}}` be the $`\sigma(M, M_*)`-closure of
$`\mathcal{A}`. Then $`\overline{\mathcal{A}}` is a $`W^*`-subalgebra of $`M`;  it is called
the $`W^*`-subalgebra of $`M` generated by $`H`. Moreover, $`\overline{\mathcal{A}}` is
commutative if $`\mathcal{A}` is commutative.
:::

:::lemma_ "lem:wstar_of_masa" (uses := "cor:sig_clos_of_cstar_is_wstar_Sak_1_7_9")
A maximal abelian $`*`-subalgebra $`\mathcal{A}` of a $`W^*`-algebra $`M` is also a
$`W^*`-algebra.
:::

:::proof "lem:wstar_of_masa"
By maximality, $`\mathcal{A} = \overline{\mathcal{A}}`, and so
{bpref "cor:sig_clos_of_cstar_is_wstar_Sak_1_7_9"}[] implies $`\mathcal{A}` is a
$`W^*`-subalgebra of $`M`.
:::

:::proposition "prop:one_sided_ideals_in_wstar_proj_Sak_1_10_1" (uses := "lem:star_left_mul_right_mul_sig_cts_Sak_1_7_8, cor:sig_clos_of_cstar_is_wstar_Sak_1_7_9, lem:proj_sub_pos_iff_comm_eq_self, lem:wstar_unital")
(Sakai 1.10.1) Let $`\mathcal{L}` (resp. $`\mathcal{R}`) be a left (resp. right)
$`\sigma(M, M_*)`-closed ideal of a $`W^*`-algebra $`M`. Then there exists a unique
projection $`p` (resp. $`q`) in $`M` so that $`\mathcal{L} = Mp` (resp.
$`\mathcal{R} = qM`).
:::

:::proof "prop:one_sided_ideals_in_wstar_proj_Sak_1_10_1"
Suppose $`\mathcal{L}` is a $`\sigma`-closed left ideal. By
{bpref "lem:star_left_mul_right_mul_sig_cts_Sak_1_7_8"}[], $`\mathcal{L}^*` is also
$`\sigma`-closed and $`\mathcal{N} = \mathcal{L} \cap \mathcal{L}^*` is a $`\sigma`-closed
$`*`-subalgebra of $`M`, and therefore by
{bpref "cor:sig_clos_of_cstar_is_wstar_Sak_1_7_9"}[] is a $`W^*`-subalgebra of $`M`. Let
$`p` be the identity of $`\mathcal{N}`, which exists by {bpref "lem:wstar_unital"}[]. Since
$`\mathcal{L}` is a left ideal, $`xp \in \mathcal{L}` for any $`x \in M`, thus
$`Mp \subseteq \mathcal{L}`; conversely, $`xp = x` for any $`x \in \mathcal{L}`, since $`p`
is the identity of $`\mathcal{N}`, thus $`\mathcal{L} \subseteq Mp`.

If $`p_1` is another projection in $`M` such that $`\mathcal{L} = Mp_1` then $`Mp = Mp_1` and
$`p_1 = xp` for some $`x \in M`. By the self-adjointness of $`p_1` we have
$`p_1 = px^* xp` and therefore $`p_1 p = p p_1 = p_1`, and therefore $`p_1 \le p` by
{bpref "lem:proj_sub_pos_iff_comm_eq_self"}[]. We get $`p \le p_1` using the same argument.
The claim for right ideals follows mutatis mutandis.
:::

:::proposition "prop:proj_compl_lat_wstar_Sak_1_10_2" (uses := "prop:one_sided_ideals_in_wstar_proj_Sak_1_10_1, lem:proj_sub_pos_iff_comm_eq_self")
(Sakai 1.10.2) Let $`M` be a $`W^*`-algebra. Its set $`M^p` of projections is a complete
lattice with respect to $`\le`.
:::

:::proof "prop:proj_compl_lat_wstar_Sak_1_10_2"
Let $`\{e_i\}_{i \in \mathbb{I}}` be any set of projections in $`M`. Consider
$`\mathcal{L}_1`, the $`\sigma`-closed left ideal generated by
$`\{Me_i\}_{i \in \mathbb{I}}`, and $`\mathcal{L}_2 = \bigcap_{i \in \mathbb{I}} Me_i`. By
{bpref "prop:one_sided_ideals_in_wstar_proj_Sak_1_10_1"}[] there exist projections
$`e_1, e_2` in $`M` so that $`\mathcal{L}_1 = Me_1` and $`\mathcal{L}_2 = Me_2`. By
{bpref "lem:proj_sub_pos_iff_comm_eq_self"}[], we know $`e_i \le e_1` and $`e_2 \le e_i` for
any $`i \in \mathbb{I}`. (To prove the first of these, since $`e_i = xe_1` for some
$`x \in M`, we have by the self-adjointness of $`e_i` that $`e_i = e_1 x^* x e_1` and so
$`e_1 e_i = e_i e_1 = e_i` and so $`e_i \le e_1`. The other direction is analogous.)

Furthermore, if $`p \in M^p` is another projection that is an upper bound for all the $`e_i`
then $`Me_1 \subseteq Mp` since $`\mathcal{L}_1 = Me_1` is the left ideal generated by the
$`e_i`. It follows that $`e_1 \le p` by the same calculation we just did. If $`p \in M^p` is
a lower bound for all the $`e_i` then $`Mp \subseteq Me_i` for every $`i \in \mathbb{I}` and
so $`Mp \subseteq \bigcap_i Me_i = Me_2` and we obtain $`p \le e_2`. It follows that
$`e_1 = \sup(e_i)` and $`e_2 = \inf(e_i)`.
:::

# The Mackey and Strong Topologies

Sakai's proof of {bpref "thm:sigma_cts_of_normal_Sak_1_13_2"}[] compares the $`\sigma`-topology
with the strong topology, through the Mackey topology. The formalization replaces this with the
GNS argument of {bpref "lem:dominated_sigma_cts"}[]. The general theory of the Mackey topology is
nevertheless partly formalized.

:::definition "def:Mackey_top" (lean := "Mackey") (uses := "def:weak_top")
Let $`B : E \times F \to \mathbb{K}` be a pairing. The locally convex topology on $`E` generated by
the seminorms $`p_K(x) = \sup_{y \in K} |B(x, y)|`, ranging over the $`\sigma(F, E)`-compact
absolutely convex subsets $`K` of $`F`, is called the *Mackey topology* on $`E`, and is denoted
$`\tau(E, F)`. For a $`W^*`-algebra $`M` this gives the Mackey topology $`\tau(M, M_*)`.
:::

:::theorem "thm:mackey_arens" (lean := "Mackey.range_coeLM_eq_range_bilin, Module.dualPairing_flip_polar_polar") (uses := "def:Mackey_top, def:compatible_dual, thm:bipolar")
(Mackey–Arens, first half) If $`B` separates the points of $`F`, the Mackey topology
$`\tau(E, F)` is compatible with $`B`.
:::

:::proof "thm:mackey_arens"
A functional continuous for $`\tau(E, F)` is bounded by $`1` on the polar of some
$`\sigma(F, E)`-compact absolutely convex set $`K`, so it lies in the bipolar of $`K` computed in
the algebraic dual of $`E`. A variant of {bpref "thm:bipolar"}[] (Voigt, *Topological Vector
Spaces*, Lemma 5.3) identifies that bipolar with $`K` itself, since $`K` is compact. Conversely,
each $`B(\cdot, y)` is $`\tau(E, F)`-continuous, taking $`K` to be the closed absolutely convex hull
of $`\{y\}`.
:::

The second half of the Mackey–Arens theorem, that $`\tau(E, F)` is the finest locally convex
topology on $`E` compatible with $`B`, is not yet formalized.

:::definition "def:strong_top" (uses := "def:wstar_def")
The locally convex topology on $`M` generated by the seminorms
$`x \mapsto \varphi(x^* x)^{1/2}` ranging over positive $`\varphi \in M_*` is called the *strong
topology*, or $`s`-*topology*, on $`M`. We denote it by $`s(M, M_*)`.
:::

By $`A \le B` on topologies below, we mean that $`A` is weaker than $`B`.

:::theorem "thm:sigma_le_s_le_tau" (uses := "def:Mackey_top, thm:mackey_arens, def:strong_top, def:sigma_top, prop:extr_sa_sa_unitaries_Sak_1_6_3")
(Sakai 1.8.9) We have $`\sigma(M, M_*) \le s(M, M_*) \le \tau(M, M_*)`.
:::

:::corollary "cor:sigma_eq_s_on_ball" (uses := "thm:sigma_le_s_le_tau")
The $`\sigma`-topology is equivalent to the $`s`-topology on the unit ball of $`M`, and hence
on bounded sets.
:::

:::lemma_ "lem:bdd_sigma_cts_iff_bdd_s_cts_Sak_1_8_10" (uses := "def:sigma_top, cor:sigma_eq_s_on_ball")
(Sakai 1.8.10) A linear functional $`\rho` on $`M` is $`\sigma`-continuous on the unit sphere
(hence $`\sigma`-continuous) if and only if it is $`s`-continuous on the unit sphere (hence
$`s`-continuous).
:::
